<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothair I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/795">795</a>–<a href="/years/855">855 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Lothair</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lothair I was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> and Carolingian emperor from 840 to 855. He fought his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 he received the imperial title and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a>, a division that marked the breakup of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothair-i.jpg" alt="Lothair I" width="272" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothar_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lothair was born in 795 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, then king of Aquitaine and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, and Ermengarde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a>. Little is known of his youth. After Charlemagne's death in 814 he was sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> for his father, the new emperor. In 817 Louis drew up the Ordinatio Imperii, which designated Lothair as his principal heir and overlord of his younger brothers Pepin of Aquitaine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, and he was crowned joint emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>.</p><p>When Bernard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> died in 818, after plotting against Louis and being blinded, Italy was awarded to Lothair. He received the royal crown of Italy at Easter 823, 5 April, and was crowned emperor again by Pope Paschal I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In November 824 he promulgated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-romana">Constitutio Romana</a> on relations between pope and emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His stepmother Judith won his consent to a plan to secure a kingdom for her son Charles, who was given Alemannia in 829. Lothair soon changed his attitude and spent a decade in strife with his father over the division of the empire. In 830 all three brothers rebelled and deposed Louis, who was reinstated in 831 and deprived Lothair of the imperial title, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to Charles. A second rebellion in 833, instigated by Angilbert II, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, deposed Louis again in 834. Through the loyalty of the Lombards Lothair kept Italy and his imperial position.</p><p>When Louis was dying in 840 he sent the imperial insignia to Lothair, who claimed the whole empire. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and half-brother Charles resisted and allied against him. At Fontenay-en-Puisaye on 25 June 841 Lothair was defeated and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>.</p><p>Peace talks began, and in June 842 the brothers met on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a>, signed in August 843, gave Lothair the imperial title, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and a long stretch of territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> along the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Provence. He soon ceded Italy to his eldest son Louis. In his new kingdom he made futile efforts to defend his lands from attacks by the Northmen and the Saracens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 855 Lothair fell seriously ill, renounced the throne, divided his lands among his three sons and entered the monastery of Prüm on 23 September. He died six days later and was buried there; his remains were found in 1860. By the Treaty of Prüm Louis II received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the title of emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> received Lotharingia, and Charles received Provence. The struggles among the brothers led directly to the breakup of the empire assembled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and laid the foundation for the development of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lothair I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was constantly renegotiating the division of the empire among <b>Lothair</b>, Pepin, Louis the German and, after 823, his youngest son Charles the Bald.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…ruler from 814, issued the Ordinatio imperii of 817 to keep the empire united under his eldest son, <b>Lothar I</b>, but the birth of another son, Charles the Bald, in 823 and attempts to provide for him provoked rebellions by his elder sons in 830 and 833, when…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis the German and Charles the Bald pledged alliance against their brother <b>Lothair</b>, each swearing in the other's language.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis, eldest son of Emperor <b>Lothair I</b>, was crowned king and co-emperor at Rome by Pope Sergius II, a ceremony mirroring Lothair's own crowning.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles the Bald and Louis the German defeated their brother <b>Lothair I</b> and Pepin II of Aquitaine at Fontenoy, the largest battle of the Carolingian civil war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-25">25 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the death of his father <b>Lothair</b>, Louis V became sole king.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Altdorf, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Prüm, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.57,12.12&amp;year=834" aria-label="Altdorf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4831 466 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lothair I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5281 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5281 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.57,12.12&amp;year=834">What was here in 834 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
